      "plan": {
        "A": "Develop an algorithmic candidate: build first-ID lookup and each node’s exact selected successor, then resolve functional DAG paths with float memoization/path compression instead of repeated recursive scans. Preserve source semantics: strict positive max selection, first matching ID, input neighbor order, float arithmetic, query order, and `%f` output.",
        "B": "Develop an independent parsing/layout candidate. Replace costly repeated `fscanf`/linear ID searches with bounded line/token parsing, compact neighbor storage, and a first-occurrence ID hash/map; pair it with a simple semantics-equivalent successor/cost resolver. Preserve all valid header, query-list, missing-neighbor, and output behavior—no input-specific shortcuts.",
        "C": "Research conditional 4-core variants on top of a correct serial optimized design: parallel successor construction, independent query evaluation with ordered emission, or dependency-safe reverse-functional-DAG evaluation. Measure overhead carefully: the baseline is only 12.002 ms, so OpenMP startup, synchronization, and output contention may lose. Never use an unsynchronized shared memo table.",
        "G": "Integrate only candidates that independently pass correctness and improve full-program measurements. Compare serial algorithm/layout variants before selectively adding parallelism; gains are not additive. Recheck exact float/output behavior and broad valid inputs, retain the fastest measured complete program, and do not alter harnesses, compiler/budgets, tests, or input-specific computation.",
        "analysis": "The source repeatedly performs linear `find_by_id` searches while scanning every chosen path, and recomputes paths for every requested ID. The strongest general opportunity is successor precomputation plus memoized functional-DAG evaluation, with fast ID lookup and parsing as complementary improvements. Preserve the reference’s observable selection and float behavior because correctness is judged against it. The 4-core target permits parallel experiments, but the 12 ms baseline makes low-overhead serial improvements the priority."
